{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments ["<b>And the sojourn of the children of Israel.</b> There are many calculations in Scripture whose basis we do not know; I shall mention some of them. \"And it came to pass at the end of forty years, and Absalom said\" (2 Samuel 15:7) — Absalom was not forty years old, nor was the kingdom of the house of David complete. The commentators said this calculation is in Egypt. After the rest of the sojourns, that they dwelt as strangers from the day the Ark rested in Kiriath-jearim. And it is written, \"And it came to pass in the thirtieth year\" (Ezekiel 1:1) — some say he began to prophesy at thirty; some say it refers to the Jubilee; some say to the finding of the Torah scroll that Hilkiah the priest found. I do not know, for these are assumptions without foundation or support. And it is written, \"Yet within sixty-five years\" (Isaiah 7:8); this prophecy was in the days of Ahaz, whose reign was sixteen years, and in the sixth year of Hezekiah his son Samaria was exiled. Thus the commentators were forced to say that the start of the sixty-five years is from the day Amos prophesied, \"Israel shall surely go into exile from off his land\" (Amos 7:11). Others said from the year of the earthquake. And when we examined the years of Israel's coming to Egypt in the days of Joseph and his brothers, the number does not reach the days of the verse, for there is no doubt that Kehath was among those who went down to Egypt. We reckon that he begot Amram his son at the end of his life, and Amram begot Moses at the end of his life — the sum of their lives is two hundred and seventy years, and Moses was eighty years old when Israel went out of Egypt. The total is three hundred and fifty years. We are short eighty, and the elders added \"which they dwelt in Egypt and in other lands.\" For \"and they shall serve them, and they shall afflict them four hundred years\" (Genesis 15:13) does not attach to \"and they shall afflict them\" but to \"your seed shall be,\" and many like it, and in this parashah, \"from the first day until the seventh day\" (Exodus 13:7 [sic]). And after we find written that HaShem said to Abraham between the pieces that \"your seed shall be a stranger four hundred years,\" we know that the calculation of the sojourn of the children of Israel depends on the covenant of Abraham. The author of Seder Olam was forced to say that after Abraham came to the land of Canaan he returned to Haran. The commentators were forced to say that the prophecy \"I am YHWH who brought you out of Ur of the Chaldees\" (Genesis 15:7) is not attached to the aforementioned parashah but was said after it. There is no need for any of this; rather, the reckoning of four hundred years is from the day Isaac was born, and these thirty additional years are counted from the day Abraham went out from the land of his birth, from Ur of the Chaldees, and came with his father to Haran, as it is written, \"and they came to Haran and dwelt there\" (Genesis 11:31). Abraham dwelt with his father in Haran five years, and came to the land of Canaan when he was seventy-five years old, and did not return to Haran. When Isaac was born, thirty years had passed since Abraham left his land, for this is the fundamental point: to him the land of the Canaanite was given, and the verse \"I am YHWH who brought you out of Ur of the Chaldees\" (Genesis 15:7) attaches to the entire parashah above and below. The meaning of \"the sojourn of the children of Israel who dwelt in Egypt\" is that they were strangers there; their fathers too were strangers, as it is written, \"like a sojourner and a stranger\" (Psalm 105:12), and Abraham said, \"I am a stranger and a sojourner with you\" (Genesis 23:4)." "<b>Thirty years and four hundred years.</b> From the day Abraham their father went out from the land of his birth."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra cites Exodus 13:7 as 'בפרשה הזאת' but the verse about eating matzot seven days fits 12:15 or 13:6 better; rendered as printed."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:34:07.400464236Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}